B1. Dynamic Student Record System 

Design a struct Student with fields: roll_no (int), name (char[20]), marks (float).
Write a C program that:
a. Asks the user for n (number of students), and dynamically allocates an array of n Student structures using malloc.
b. Reads all n records from the user.
c. Computes and prints the average marks, and the record of the student with the highest marks, using a pointer to traverse the array (not array-indexing — use pointer arithmetic, e.g. (ptr + i)).
d. Frees the allocated memory before exiting.
e. If n changes at runtime (ask the user if they want to add 2 more students), use realloc to grow the array without losing existing data.
Sample interaction:
Enter number of students: 3
Enter roll, name, marks: 1 Asha 88
Enter roll, name, marks: 2 Ravi 72
Enter roll, name, marks: 3 Meera 95

Average marks: 85.00
Topper: Meera (Roll 3, Marks 95.00)

Add 2 more students? (y/n): y
Enter roll, name, marks: 4 Kiran 60
Enter roll, name, marks: 5 Tara 91
Updated average marks: 81.20
 
<h2>B2. Employee Database using a Union</h2>

A company has two types of employees: full-time (paid a fixed monthly salary) and contract (paid hourly_rate × hours_worked). Define:
typedef struct {
    char name[20];
    int  emp_id;
    enum { FULL_TIME, CONTRACT } type;
    union {
        float salary;
        struct { float hourly_rate; int hours_worked; } contract_info;
    } pay;
} Employee;
Write a C program that:
a. Reads n employees into a dynamically allocated array of Employee.
b. For each employee, based on type, reads the correct union fields.
c. Prints a payslip for every employee, computing total pay correctly depending on the type (direct salary for full-time; hourly_rate × hours_worked for contract).
d. Prints the total salary bill of the company.
 
<h2>B3. Polynomial Addition using the Array-of-Coefficients Representation</h2>

Write a C program that:
a. Reads two polynomials from the user as (degree, list of coefficients), using the representation from class:
   typedef struct {
       int   degree;
       float coef[101];   /* coef[i] = coefficient of x^(degree-i) */
   } polynomial;
b. Implements a function polynomial add(polynomial a, polynomial b) that adds them term by term (matching exponents), without using any built-in polynomial library.
c. Implements a function void display(polynomial p) that prints the polynomial in the form 3x^4 + 2x^2 + 1 (skip terms whose coefficient is 0, and handle the constant term without printing x^0).
d. Test your program on:
•	A(x) = 3x⁴ + 2x² + 1 and B(x) = 5x³ − 2x² + 4 → expected output: 3x^4 + 5x^3 + 5
•	A case where the sum of two matching terms becomes exactly 0 (make sure your output correctly omits that term).

 
<h2>B4. Sparse Matrix: Representation + Fast Transpose</h2>

Write a C program that:
a. Reads a matrix as rows, cols, and its elements (or reads only the non-zero entries directly, your choice — state your input format clearly at the top of your code).
b. Stores it using the array-of-triples representation:
   typedef struct { int row, col, value; } term;
with a[0] as the header (rows, cols, number of non-zero terms), exactly as covered in class.
c. Implements fast_transpose(term a[], term b[]) exactly using the counting/starting-position method taught in class (you may not simply call the naive transpose, and you may not sort the output array afterward).
d. Prints:
•	The original matrix's triples.
•	The row_terms[] and starting_pos[] arrays your function computed internally (print these — this is how we'll verify you implemented the fast method and not a disguised sort).
•	The final transposed triples, and confirm they are already in row-major order.
e. Test your program on this matrix and include the output in your report:
   5 0 0
   0 0 3
   2 0 7
